5. Custom Comparators
5.1 Largest Number – LeetCode
Problem
Arrange numbers so that they form the largest possible number.
Java Solution
import java.util.*;
 
class Solution {
 
   public String largestNumber(int[] nums) {
 
       String[] arr = new String[nums.length];
 
       for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
           arr[i] = String.valueOf(nums[i]);
       }
 
       Arrays.sort(arr, (a, b) ->
           (b + a).compareTo(a + b)
       );
 
       if (arr[0].equals("0")) {
           return "0";
       }
 
       StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
 
       for (String s : arr) {
           result.append(s);
       }
 
       return result.toString();
   }
}
Example
Input:
[10, 2]
 
Output:
"210"
Because:
2 + 10 = "210"
10 + 2 = "102"
Therefore, 2 should come before 10.
Another Example
Input:
[3,30,34,5,9]
 
Output:
"9534330"
Main Comparator Logic
(b + a).compareTo(a + b)
This decides which number should appear first.

6. Java Comparator – HackerRank
Java Solution
import java.util.*;
 
class Student {
   int id;
   String name;
   double cgpa;
 
   Student(int id, String name, double cgpa) {
       this.id = id;
       this.name = name;
       this.cgpa = cgpa;
   }
}
 
class Checker implements Comparator<Student> {
 
   public int compare(Student a, Student b) {
 
       // Higher CGPA first
       int result = Double.compare(b.cgpa, a.cgpa);
 
       if (result != 0)
           return result;
 
       // Alphabetical name
       result = a.name.compareTo(b.name);
 
       if (result != 0)
           return result;
 
       // Lower ID first
       return Integer.compare(a.id, b.id);
   }
}
Sorting Priority
1. Higher CGPA
2. Alphabetical name
3. Lower ID
For example:
Student A → CGPA 9.2
Student B → CGPA 8.9
Student C → CGPA 8.9
Student A comes first because of the highest CGPA.

